  "text": "Question five. How do you estimate the cost of an LLM feature? Tokens, not requests, are the unit: daily requests, times the tokens in and out of each, times their prices, which are quoted per million tokens and differ for input and output. The page's example: a million requests a day, each with two thousand input tokens and three hundred output. Its prices are illustrative, a dollar per million input tokens and five dollars per million output. Check your own provider's. Input comes to two thousand dollars a day, and output to fifteen hundred. That's thirty-five hundred a day, about a hundred and five thousand a month. The breakdown says which lever to pull. Input is the larger share, so trimming the prompt, caching the shared prefix or retrieving fewer documents saves more than shortening the answer. After that, route easy requests to a smaller model, and send offline work to a discounted batch API. But here an output token costs five times an input token, so why not cut the answer? Because there are far fewer of them to cut. Halve the prompt, and you save a thousand dollars a day. Halve the answer, seven hundred and fifty.",
